{"id":48,"course_id":12,"title":"Plan Review, Drift, and Safe Changes","summary":"Turn terraform plan into an engineering review artifact.","sequence":4,"body":"## Mental model\nA plan is not a rubber stamp. Replacements, broad IAM policy changes, route changes, and security group openings need explicit review and rollback thinking.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice spotting replace/destroy actions, unknown values, sensitive outputs, and drift signals.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nWrite a plan review summary that calls out blast radius, rollback, validation, and cost impact.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-terraform-aws-infrastructure","course_category":"Terraform","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":236,"term":"Terraform for AWS Platform Infrastructure","context":"Terraform","definition":"Learn reproducible infrastructure workflows for VPCs, EKS modules, state, plans, reviews, drift, and environment promotion."},{"id":237,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":238,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":239,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":240,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":142,"lesson_id":48,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Plan Review, Drift, and Safe Changes?","answer":"Turn terraform plan into an engineering review artifact.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":143,"lesson_id":48,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":144,"lesson_id":48,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
